## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical memory-management skills: implementing per-thread caching, lock-free global pools, ABA avoidance, and handling thread cleanup.

## Key Concepts
- **Thread-local caches**: Minimize contention by keeping allocations per-thread
- **Lock-free free-list**: Use CAS (compare-and-swap) for thread-safe global pool access
- **ABA avoidance**: Tagged pointers or epoch-based schemes prevent the ABA problem where a recycled pointer is misinterpreted as valid
- **Burst refills**: Refill local caches in batches to reduce global pool access frequency
- **Cache locality**: Keeping objects per-thread improves CPU cache hit rates
- **Safe cleanup**: Handle releasing per-thread buffers back to the global pool when threads exit

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Block { struct Block *next; char pad[56]; } Block;

typedef struct { _Atomic(Block *) head; } FreeList;

static inline void* pool_alloc(FreeList *g, _Atomic(Block *) *local, int *lcnt) {
    if (*lcnt == 0) {
        Block *batch[32];
        Block *head = atomic_load_explicit(&g->head, memory_order_acquire);
        do { for (int i = 0; i < 31; i++) batch[i] = batch[i]->next; } 
        while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&g->head, &head, batch[31]));
        *lcnt = 31;
        return batch[0];
    }
    return local[--*lcnt];
}

static inline void pool_free(FreeList *g, _Atomic(Block *) *local, int *lcnt, void *ptr) {
    if (*lcnt == 32) {
        Block *head = atomic_load_explicit(&g->head, memory_order_relaxed);
        do { local[31] = head; } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&g->head, &head, local[0]));
        *lcnt = 0;
    }
    local[(*lcnt)++] = ptr;
}
```

